KNOWNBUG
break_assertion_after_loop1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.0\.p0\] always 0: REFUTED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
An assertion placed after a for loop whose body contains a `break` must be
checked normally; `break` only terminates the loop (IEEE 1800-2017, 12.8),
it does not make the code following the loop unreachable. The loop runs
every cycle and breaks at i==2, so `p0: assert(0)` after the loop is reached
on every clock cycle and must be REFUTED.

Observed: ebmc reports `[main.0.p0] always 0: PROVED up to bound 2`, i.e. the
assertion is vacuously proved.

Root cause: verilog_rtl_buildert::build_statement in
src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp implements `break` by pushing false_exprt onto
state.guard to kill the rest of the path. When the enclosing `if` has a
constant condition, build_if executes the branch directly on `state`, so the
dead (false) guard is never popped; it stays active after the loop and makes
every later assertion in the always block vacuous.
